Hi OMG! Ubuntu team,

a desktop app tip that fits your coverage of native Linux software:
Solidon3D 0.3.3 has just been released, with Linux as a first-class
platform: Flatpak and AppImage, same feature set as Windows and macOS.

The one-line pitch: fix a downloaded STL without CAD. Click a mounting hole
that sits in the wrong place and move it. Position and axis are preserved,
the change stays as an undoable history step, and the plate goes straight to
your existing slicer. This release is the one that made features properly
editable: a recognised hole, peg, countersink or dome can now be moved,
rotated, resized, duplicated and removed, where before only the diameter
could change.

Points your readers tend to care about:

* fully offline after a one-time device activation: no account, no
  cloud project store, projects are plain local files;
* one-time purchase, no subscription; the complete demo runs free until
  30 October 2026;
* the optional AI assistant can run entirely locally against Ollama,
  and it only calls the same documented operations a click does -
  geometry is always computed by code, never by the model;
* a new default view navigation with W/A/S/D flight, while the familiar
  Cura, Bambu Studio, Orca, PrusaSlicer, CAD and Blender schemes stay
  selectable;
* localised into six languages, manual included.

Download and overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Changelog: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

Happy to provide screenshots, a Flatpak/AppImage test build walkthrough,
or a before/after STL example if useful.

